the task of making order victorious over  chaos or randomness if you take this  seriously you will look upon it as  warfare Alan Watts was a 20th century  British American philosopher born in  1915 he's renowned for his wit eloquence  and success in translating and infusing  Eastern philosophy into the Western  world wasas regarded as being greatly  responsible for the popularization of  Buddhist and Zen principles in the West  inspiring the beat and hippie movements  in the United States during the 60s and  70s and causing major cultural shifts  away from traditional Western values  during his life before dying in 1973 at  age 58 Watts published over 25 books and  articles on various Western and Eastern  theories and was the first to write a  best-selling book on Buddhism entitled  the way of Zen published in 1957 Watts  his unique ability to understand  conceive and translate complex  alternative philosophies into practical  digestible language makes it clear why  he is one of the most enjoyed and  influential philosophers in recent  history Watts is interest in Eastern  philosophy stemmed from his childhood  enjoyment of Chinese and Japanese  storybooks as well as his affinity with  Eastern aesthetic he found that things  like light color and nature were shown  and profoundly beautiful and provoking  waves within Eastern art and he wondered  what it was that allowed these artists  to see and consider the world with such  lucidity Watts was born in what is now  South East London and was initially  raised Christian however during Watts is  developing younger years he began to  find that Christianity's methods of  understanding and dealing with life left  him unappeased unfulfilled and often  with more problems than solutions in  Watson's mind Christianity did not  attempt in any real sense to answer  things like what the true nature of the  universe is what the self is what the  meaning of life is and how to deal with  it all rather Christianity appeared more  interested in studying and maintaining  history and remained firmly transfixed  on the idea that there is some  tyrannical like God figure controlling  everything this idea of an authority  figure controlling the universe was both  unappealing and insensible to Watts and  so in combination with his growing  fondness and curiosity with the eastern  aesthetic he would embark on his journey  into Buddhism  what drew Watts further into Buddhism  was that in Buddhism rather than a  tyrannical like god controlling  everything with some rational or moral  intention there is a universal  consciousness which everything is an  interconnected and interdependent part  of a singular essence to life that  follows its own nature with no boss  every object and every person is merely  an expression of this universal nature  following the nature of itself for the  mere sake of itself Watts found this  concept to be far more reasonable humane  and useful to the experience of life as  Watts would study consider and practice  Eastern ideas like this one he found  that he experienced a certain bliss in  their realization often regarded as  enlightenment or awakening life became  richer anxieties and fears reduced if  not disappeared and peace and connection  with nature blossomed it was this that  Watts wanted to share with the Westerner  an individual whom Watts felt deeply  needed a way out or in  depending on where he stood with a  distinctively diverse background in  understanding of both Western and  Eastern worlds from a cultural  standpoint Watts found that much of the  Western world was intensely alienated  and conflicted often obsessed with  status wealth consumption and success in  being right or the best yet even when  one was successful in the Western ideal  of success they still appeared to remain  lost and disconnected from themselves  their fellow human and nature itself the  idea that a successful business a new  luxury product sold or bought a higher  status a social power or anything alike  could resolve the human experience of  isolation anxiety and vulnerability was  exceptionally foolish in Watsons mind  furthermore it appeared that the Western  religious idea of an absolute morality  and an authoritative God did not really  assist the westerner and his or her  ability to resolve these behaviors or  help discover any spiritual essence in  themselves rather it only further detach  them from the physical of metaphysical  world but making them a subject of  everything instead of a part of  everything the principles of Zen and  watch's teachings diverged rather  significantly as humans we have a very  peculiar consciousness that is aware of  what it is perceiving and furthermore  identifies with its awareness this  self-identification is generally  regarded as ego and as a result of our  ego we tend to feel as though we are  detached from what we are observing that  if there is an i or me of  serving then I must not be what I am  observing that my internal must be  separate from my external Watts would  often refer to this as the sense that we  are an ego in a bag of skin however in  Zen in Watsons teachings this concept  and feeling is not only incorrect but it  is the source of most of our alienation  anxiety suffering and subsequent  animosity with the knowledge that we  will die that things will change that  stuff will dissolve how can we derive  any sense of meaning or connection to  life by chaining our sense of self to  these things to our body to our personal  identity to our stuff to our status one  of if not the most fundamental principle  in Zen and Watson's teachings is the  idea that attempts to resolve this  problem the idea that we are none of  these things that we tend to identify  with but instead we are everything and  everything is us human and nature go  together as one linked inexorably in  infinitely just as noise can't exist  without silence just as light can exist  without darkness our internal cannot  exist without our external and our  experience cannot exist without our non  experience or death everything needs  everything else in order to be anything  at all watts would often define this  using the term organism environment  meaning we are not an organism in an  environment but we are an organism and  an environment simultaneously we are but  a feature of the whole and thus we are  the whole for Watts the illusion that  much of the Western world has and  continues to live in is that our  thoughts are exempt from the laws of the  universe that we have a separate  volition detached from nature and our  thoughts and actions occur voluntarily  and can impose themselves on nature  however for Watts the truth is that our  brain and our consciousness are not  exempt or separate not from the cause  and effect process of the universe and  not from the laws that everything  operates on within the Watson Zen school  of thought success in life is not to  change the world or to make a certain  amount of wealth or to surpass anyone  and status or power or anything of that  sort but the ultimate goal and success  in life is to become deeply synchronized  with nature itself to realize that your  behavior is one with the behavior of the  rest of the world a person is in fact  nature itself and so a person cannot  choose to change or influence nature  because even what appears to be a human  effect on  nature is only in essence a nature  effect on nature which is to say  everything is just nature playing the  game of nature with us and through us  like a river that flows without choosing  to or how to we humans move and act  without choosing to or how to we did not  choose to be a human or the kind of  human we are we did not choose the  conditions of the human body or mind we  simply express the nature of the  universe through them and the  achievement of Zen is to flow freely  without choosing acknowledging that one  is and has always been without choosing  to accept this and overcome one's ego is  no small feat it is to destroy one sense  of self-importance which is of course  very important to the thing that  believes and wants so very badly to be  important but the irony is that perhaps  what we think we want is not what we  need perhaps thinking we are more or  less important than anything or anyone  is not all that helpful for the  experience of life perhaps alternatively  realizing one's unimportance as the  potential to actually open up the door  to a whole new realm of meaningfulness  and enjoyable Ness like an artist in a  state of flow instead of forcing nature  we synchronize with it and perform life  with fluidity and ease of course Watts  is not without criticism nor are the  Eastern philosophies that he  communicated and derived many of his  ideas from it is reasonable to find some  of watch's teachings and translations to  be either confusing or fuzzy for ease up  against a very difficult opponent be it  the very thing we have been conditioned  and wired to identify life with even  four Watts himself it is fair to say  that the ideas he shared were not easy  and did not resolve all the challenges  of being human for him according to  those who knew him he was not without  struggle in her conflict or vice perhaps  his take on life is accurate and  complete or perhaps it is not and  perhaps that is not the point at this  time in our evolution we can only really  speculate what the true nature of  everything is there is no answer at the  back of the book that we can peek at  when things get confusing however that  does not mean that efforts and attempts  at speculating and understanding our  spiritual essence is without value  perhaps it is not the exact accuracy of  any philosophical idea that is important  what the idea can do for us for many  watts as ideas and offerings have and  continue to radically shift how they see  and experience life and a positive  practical  that Christianity and other traditional  Western ideologies continue to overlook  it is not only what Watts said but also  how he said it and who he said it to in  part Watson's fame can be attributed to  his unique ability to translate and  communicate a way out of the cycle of  consumption anxiety and alienation in a  part of the world that has and continues  to severely suffer from these things for  Watts and those who agree with him his  teachings suggest very comforting and  useful ideas of life that fundamentally  there are no mistakes no failures no  successes no good or bad things rather  there are just things that happen that  contrast with other things that happen  that life is not a contest with winners  and losers but an endless game of  back-and-forth that we are not separate  from everything but we are an expression  of everything forever linked with what  has and will always be and that we don't  need to take things quite so seriously  we're an ordinary person sees a battle  an extraordinary person sees a  cooperation a dance as Watts would often  call it and there is no real point to a  dance other than to mystify and  entertain which is to say everything and  everyone is all working together at once  to mystify and entertain to express the  single thing that is universal  consciousness that is life and here we  are in the stance with an ability to  form reason trying to make sense of this  apparently senseless dance and our  attempts we become confused and seek  better meaning better justification  better understanding we overanalyze and  take ownership in this dance in order to  compensate for its elusiveness we  struggle to accept it for what it is but  perhaps there is no better sense to  anything than senselessness for in the  senselessness there is wonder  mystification and entertainment and  perhaps it is this idea that remains  true no matter what we believe in life  whether we agree with Watts Zen  Christianity or any of the other  countless religions and ideologies we're  almost certainly wrong or incomplete in  our understanding and so for now perhaps  the best we can do is to try and enjoy  the dance for what it is in all of its  senselessness and remember that the only  thing that could be bad is to take it  seriously Alan Watts  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

